    "sql_snippets": {
      "measures": [
        {
          "display_name": "net sales",
          "id": "01f1b6b606f9116ab5184d3d95b8b70d",
          "instruction": [
            "Use this measure as the main sales metric when users ask about\n",
            "net sales, total sales, sales amount, or revenue."
          ],
          "sql": [
            "SUM(fact_sales.net_sales_amount)"
          ],
          "synonyms": [
            "net sales total net sales sales sales amount revenue"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    "text_instructions": [
      {
        "content": [
          "This Genie Space is designed for Retail Sales and Customer Analytics.\r\n",
          "\r\n",
          "The main data model is a star schema in the Gold layer.\r\n",
          "\r\n",
          "Use these tables:\r\n",
          "- fact_sales for sales and transaction metrics.\r\n",
          "- dim_customer for customer information.\r\n",
          "- dim_product for product information.\r\n",
          "- dim_date for date and time analysis.\r\n",
          "\r\n",
          "General rules:\r\n",
          "\r\n",
          "1. Use fact_sales as the main table for sales analysis.\r\n",
          "\r\n",
          "2. Use dim_customer when the question is related to customers, customer type,\r\n",
          "location, industry, annual revenue, or number of employees.\r\n",
          "\r\n",
          "3. Use dim_product when the question is related to products, categories,\r\n",
          "subcategories, brands, or product performance.\r\n",
          "\r\n",
          "4. Use dim_date for monthly, yearly, quarterly, and other time-based analysis.\r\n",
          "\r\n",
          "5. When a question requires information from different tables,\r\n",
          "join the fact table with the corresponding dimension table using the\r\n",
          "available keys.\r\n",
          "\r\n",
          "6. For sales or revenue questions, use net sales as the main sales metric\r\n",
          "unless the user explicitly asks for another metric.\r\n",
          "\r\n",
          "7. Do not use product unit price as total sales.\r\n",
          "Use the sales value stored in fact_sales.\r\n",
          "\r\n",
          "8. When showing monetary values, use two decimal places.\r\n",
          "\r\n",
          "9. For trends over time, sort the result chronologically.\r\n",
          "\r\n",
          "10. For Top N questions, sort the requested metric in descending order.\r\n",
          "\r\n",
          "11. Do not invent values or business information that is not available\r\n",
          "in the tables.\r\n",
          "\r\n",
          "12. If the question is ambiguous and cannot be answered correctly from\r\n",
          "the available data, explain what information is missing.\r\n",
          "\r\n",
          "13. Do not speculate about the causes of changes, anomalies, increases,\r\n",
          "decreases, or unusual values in the data.\r\n",
          "\r\n",
          "Only describe conclusions that can be directly supported by the available data.\r\n",
          "\r\n",
          "Never assume that a low or unusual value means incomplete data, missing data,\r\n",
          "a business disruption, seasonality, an error, or another cause unless there\r\n",
          "is explicit evidence for that explanation in the available data.\r\n",
          "\r\n",
          "If the cause of a change cannot be determined from the available data,\r\n",
          "do not suggest a possible cause. Simply report the observed result.\r\n",
          "\r\n",
          "For example:\r\n",
          "Correct: \"May 2026 recorded the lowest net sales at $13,253.\"\r\n",
          "Incorrect: \"May 2026 recorded only $13,253, suggesting incomplete data.\""
        ],
        "id": "01f1b6b4d3401aa09723e21dbe867ec0"
      }
    ]
  },
